[MKISTM - 2026-03-30T14:30:03Z]
Hello @L-proger,

Thank you for your report, I believe that current behavior is correct and the documentation ([Link](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32h7xx-hal-driver/blob/a1996eed9172b59887bafaaa0ea1816ea14d48b5/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_dma.c#L1344)) that explain current approach has already existed in our DMA driver.

moreover, according to our [RM](https://www.st.com/resource/en/reference_manual/rm0399-stm32h745755-and-stm32h747757-advanced-armbased-32bit-mcus-stmicroelectronics.pdf) you can find **CT** bit descriptions:
> Bit 19 CT: Current target (only in double-buffer mode)
> This bit is set and cleared by hardware. It can also be written by software.
> 0: Current target memory is memory 0 (addressed by the DMA_SxM0AR pointer).
> 1: Current target memory is memory 1 (addressed by the DMA_SxM1AR pointer).
> This bit can be written only if EN = 0 to indicate the target memory area of the first transfer. 
> Once the stream is enabled, this bit operates as a status flag indicating which memory area 
> is the current target.

[ruddy17 - 2026-03-30T15:36:02Z]
@MKISTM but this doesn't make sense. The quote you provided from RM directly contradicts the code you referenced :/

[L-proger - 2026-03-30T15:39:45Z]
> Hello [@L-proger](https://github.com/L-proger),
> 
> Thank you for your report, I believe that current behavior is correct and the documentation ([Link](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32h7xx-hal-driver/blob/a1996eed9172b59887bafaaa0ea1816ea14d48b5/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_dma.c#L1344)) that explain current approach has already existed in our DMA driver.
> 
> moreover, according to our [RM](https://www.st.com/resource/en/reference_manual/rm0399-stm32h745755-and-stm32h747757-advanced-armbased-32bit-mcus-stmicroelectronics.pdf) you can find **CT** bit descriptions:
> 
> > Bit 19 CT: Current target (only in double-buffer mode)
> > This bit is set and cleared by hardware. It can also be written by software.
> > 0: Current target memory is memory 0 (addressed by the DMA_SxM0AR pointer).
> > 1: Current target memory is memory 1 (addressed by the DMA_SxM1AR pointer).
> > This bit can be written only if EN = 0 to indicate the target memory area of the first transfer.
> > Once the stream is enabled, this bit operates as a status flag indicating which memory area
> > is the current target.



Thanks for the reply, but this does not address the issue I reported.

I am aware of the RM description of the CT bit:

CT = 0: current target memory is Memory 0
CT = 1: current target memory is Memory 1
My point is exactly that the HAL callback dispatch logic appears inconsistent with that definition.

In the driver code:

cpp
/* Current memory buffer used is Memory 1 */
if((((DMA_Stream_TypeDef *)hdma->Instance)->CR & DMA_SxCR_CT) != 0U)
{
  if(hdma->XferM1CpltCallback != NULL)
  {
    /* Transfer complete Callback for memory1 */
    hdma->XferM1CpltCallback(hdma);
  }
}
/* Current memory buffer used is Memory 0 */
else
{
  if(hdma->XferCpltCallback != NULL)
  {
    /* Transfer complete Callback for memory0 */
    hdma->XferCpltCallback(hdma);
  }
}

This logic looks reversed.

If CT == 1, then according to the RM the DMA is currently using Memory 1.
That means the buffer that has just completed is Memory 0, so the callback for Memory 0 should be called, i.e. XferCpltCallback, not XferM1CpltCallback.

Likewise, if CT == 0, then DMA is currently using Memory 0, which means Memory 1 has just completed, so XferM1CpltCallback should be called.

So the issue is not with the RM wording.
The issue is that the HAL code seems to interpret CT as “the buffer that completed”, while the RM defines it as “the current target buffer”.

Please clarify this specific point:

why is the callback selected from the current target buffer rather than the buffer that has just become free?
or, if this is intentional, what is the expected software model for safely identifying the completed buffer in double-buffer mode?
At the moment, the implementation and the RM description do not appear consistent.

[MKISTM - 2026-04-01T09:25:10Z]
Hello @L-proger,

I would like to draw your attention to the descriptions of the DMA_SxM0AR_M0A & DMA_SxM1AR_M1A bits.

> the stream is enabled (EN = 1 in DMA_SxCR) and bit CT = 0 in DMA_SxCR .
> the stream is enabled (EN = 1 in DMA_SxCR) and bit CT = 1 in DMA_SxCR .

BR,
Mohamed Chaker.

[MKISTM - 2026-04-01T09:44:23Z]
See also https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32h7xx-hal-driver/pull/100

[ruddy17 - 2026-04-01T10:37:12Z]
I am sorry @MKISTM , but I have to insist, that in this case you are obviously wrong. In the #100 you were correct with your fix, as in the case of HALF COMPLETE event, the DMA stays on the same memory buffer. So the fix you applied to #100 was totally correct. I suspect that you decided to erroneously employ the same logic and preemptively fixed the COMPLETE callbacks, which didn't need fixing. Unlike the HALF COMPLETE, when we reach full COMPLETE DMA event, it means, that the memory buffers **already have switched**! So let me try to show you again the flaw in your logic by providing once again the quote from hal driver, with some added comments from me for clarity:

// This is copied from the NEW version of HAL. I am trying to prove it is wrong. 
/* Current memory buffer used is Memory 1 */
if((((DMA_Stream_TypeDef *)hdma->Instance)->CR & DMA_SxCR_CT) != 0U)     //     if CT=1 
{
  if(hdma->XferM1CpltCallback != NULL)
  {
    /* Transfer complete Callback for memory1 */
    hdma->XferM1CpltCallback(hdma);
    // Let's see what we did:
    // So, if CT = 1, we call "DMA transfer complete Memory1" callback
    // Let's think, what would the user do in the handler of such callback?
    // I'd argue, that when Memory 1 completed, DMA switched activity to Memory 0,
    // And it is time to update Memory 1, right?
    // For this, in the callback handler we write to DMA_SxM1AR address register ...
    // ...
    // But the CT is 1!!! According to RM 16.5.9 , we are not allowed to write to DMA_SxM1AR when CT is 1!!!
    // If we try, we fall into DMA Transfer Error
  }
}
/* Current memory buffer used is Memory 0 */
else // CT=0
{
  if(hdma->XferCpltCallback != NULL)
  {
    /* Transfer complete Callback for memory0 */
    hdma->XferCpltCallback(hdma);
    // Here the same problem for the other buffer
  }
}


@MKISTM @ALABSTM please, think about this with a fresh mind. This was never a bug before the latest HAL broke it by the unnecessary change. Also, see the logic on a very similar code for BDMA : [link](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32h7xx-hal-driver/blob/a1996eed9172b59887bafaaa0ea1816ea14d48b5/Src/stm32h7xx_hal_dma.c#L1489) 
When CT=0, it correctly calls XferM1CpltCallback (and vice versa)

