[mwswt - 2026-02-11T08:13:40Z]
Hello @KRASTM 

[IV5_Inverter_H753.zip](https://github.com/user-attachments/files/25228746/IV5_Inverter_H753.zip)

I am encountering an issue with the STM32 HAL driver for DFSDM when using DMA in Normal Mode (Non-Circular).

Configuration:
    Peripheral: DFSDM with DMA.
    Mode: Normal Mode (Single Shot).
    Attachment: Please see the attached .ioc file.

Issue Description:
After a successful single-shot conversion, I am unable to simply restart the DFSDM via HAL_DFSDM_FilterRegularStart_DMA. The driver state prevents a re-trigger. Currently, the only way to restart the conversion is to completely de-initialize and re-initialize the peripheral, which is not ideal.

Observed Behavior:
I attempted to call HAL_DFSDM_FilterRegularStop_DMA inside the ConversionComplete callback (IRQ) to reset the state. However, this causes the driver to enter an Error State.
It appears that because the DMA transfer has already finished (due to Normal Mode), the Stop function treats the inactive DMA as an error condition.

Feedback:
In my opinion, this logic seems incorrect:
HAL_DFSDM_FilterRegularStop_DMA should not return an error simply because the DMA has already stopped naturally. It should handle this state gracefully.

There should be a standard way to re-trigger a Single Shot without forcing a full De-Init/Re-Init cycle or implementing manual workarounds to clear error flags.

Could you please verify if this behavior is intended or if there is a recommended standard procedure for this specific use case?

Best regards,

Markus

[KRASTM - 2026-02-16T13:47:05Z]
Hello @mwswt,

I have checked with our team, and it appears that using DMA to transfer a single data item in non-continuous mode is not a standard use case. Therefore, they recommend using DFSDM_CONTINUOUS_CONV_ON or the interrupt mode (IT). Otherwise, you may submit a post in the [ST Community](https://community.st.com/t5/stm32-mcus-software-development/ct-p/mcu-software-development-tools).

Please allow me to close this thread. Thank you for your understanding.

With regards,

[mwswt - 2026-02-18T10:10:35Z]
Hello @KRASTM,

Thank you for your clarification.

If this is indeed not a supported use case, then I will have to proceed with a workaround. Unfortunately, using continuous (cyclic) mode is not an option in our application, as we require precise control over the exact start time of each conversion.

I will evaluate the possibility of using interrupt mode; however, I am concerned that the additional interrupt load may not be acceptable for our system.

If you can confirm that this behavior is definitively not supported, I will accept that. Nevertheless, I would appreciate it if you could forward this specific use case to your experts once more, as it may not have been fully considered. For our application, it is important to be able to trigger a DFSDM conversion at a defined time and have the result transferred directly into a DMA variable.

Thank you again for your support.

