[TOUNSTM - 2025-05-08T10:25:14Z]
Hello @multiplemonomials,

I hope you are doing well. We have finally received feedback from our development teams. The DMA FIFO error is a known issue and should be ignored by applications when users do not use HAL drivers. The HAL PPP drivers (SPI/I2C/SAI/DCMI) are equipped to manage this DMA error, ensuring that no callback is triggered in the event of a DMA FIFO error. Therefore, it is recommended that users employ HAL drivers provided by STMicroelectronics.

The following code snippet illustrates how the HAL SPI driver handles DMA errors, specifically by ignoring FIFO errors:

static void SPI_DMAError(DMA_HandleTypeDef *hdma)
{
  SPI_HandleTypeDef *hspi = (SPI_HandleTypeDef *)((DMA_HandleTypeDef *)hdma)->Parent;

  /* if DMA error is FIFO error ignore it */
  if (HAL_DMA_GetError(hdma) != HAL_DMA_ERROR_FE)
  {
    /* Call SPI standard close procedure */
    SPI_CloseTransfer(hspi);

    SET_BIT(hspi->ErrorCode, HAL_SPI_ERROR_DMA);
    hspi->State = HAL_SPI_STATE_READY;
#if (USE_HAL_SPI_REGISTER_CALLBACKS == 1UL)
    hspi->ErrorCallback(hspi);
#else
    HAL_SPI_ErrorCallback(hspi);
#endif /* USE_HAL_SPI_REGISTER_CALLBACKS */
  }
}


### Note:
In **memory-to-memory mode**, the DMA manages requests and transfers internally, so FIFO overrun conditions are not detected. This simplifies the error handling process for such operations.

I hope this explanation provides clarity on the situation. Please allow me to close this issue.

Kind regards,

Title: HASH_Start_IT fails when called with buffer size < 4 while in HAL_HASH_PHASE_PROCESS phase

Body:
**Describe the set-up**
 * STM32H757I-EVAL
 * arm-none-eabi-gcc (Arch Repository) 14.1.0 + CMake

**Describe the bug**
HASH_Start_IT silently fails when called with Size < 4 while in HAL_HASH_PHASE_PROCESS phase. The interrupt handler is never called, no error returned

**How To Reproduce**
1. Indicate the global behavior of your application project
Hashing data of arbitrary length

3. The modules that you suspect to be the cause of the problem (Driver, BSP, MW ...)
stm32h7xx_hal_hash.c HASH_Start_IT

5. The use case that generates the problem
I'm reading and hashing data in chunks, the last chunk can be any size. Let's say the chunk size is 64 bytes and I want to hash 66 bytes of data. That is 64 + 2

7. How we can reproduce the problem

this should give a general idea of what I'm trying to do.
c
HASH_HandleTypeDef hhash;
/////////
void example() {
    HAL_HASH_DeInit(&hhash);
    hhash.Init.DataType = HASH_DATATYPE_8B;
    HAL_HASH_Init(&hhash);

    tx_semaphore_create(&sync, "HASH sync", 0);

    uint8_t buf[64];
    uint8_t out[32];
    memset(buf, 0, sizeof(buf));

    // chunk 1
    // This will switch the phase to HAL_HASH_PHASE_PROCESS, removing it will make the next call succeed
    HASH_Accumulate_IT(&hhash, buf, 64, HASH_ALGOSELECTION_SHA256);
    tx_semaphore_get(&sync, TX_WAIT_FOREVER);

    // chunk 2 - last
