Title: HAL_FDCAN_IRQHandler() : Concern with regards to value assigned to FDCAN_CCU->IR and FDCAN_CCU

Body:
Dear Developer,

File: STM32h7xx_hal_fdcan.C

I am concerned about the values assigned to variables TxEventFifoITs, RxFifo0Its, and RxFifo1Its.

I am concerned about the values assigned to registers FDCAN_CCU->IR and FDCAN_CCU->IE.

**Observations:**

As per the STM32H743 / STM32H753 Reference manual, the bit numbers 30 and 31 of **FDCAN_IR** is RESERVED. Similarly the bit numbers 30 and 31 of **FDCAN_IE** are also reserved.

 Also the **CCU_IE** register bits 2 through 31 are reserved.


**Concerns:**

Please refer to below declaration for CAN and the shared code parts.

I want to understand why one would call FDCAN_CCU->IE |= (((__INTERRUPT__) & CCU_IR_MASK) >> 30); when the passed __INTERRUPT__ will always have unknown value for bit 30 and bit 31?

When HAL_FDCAN_IRQHandler() function executes, a value gets  assigned to variables out of TxEventFifoITs, RxFifo0Its, and RxFifo1Its.

**My concern is that when both FDCAN_IR and FDCAN_IE have reserved bit numbers 30 and 31, then why do we want to use these bits by first copying their information to the variables and later using the values with the variables for assigning the two bits of FDCAN_CCU_IR and FDCAN_CCU_IE?**

I also observe that RxFifo1Its >> 4 is actually the value that coder will have to use inside HAL_FDCAN_RxFifo1Callback().

And that TxEventFifoITs >> 8 is actually the value that coder will have to use inside HAL_FDCAN_TxEventFifoCallback()

**MACROS:**

#define FDCAN_IR_MASK ((uint32_t)0x3FCFFFFFU) /*!< FDCAN interrupts mask */
#define CCU_IR_MASK   ((uint32_t)0xC0000000U) /*!< CCU interrupts mask   */

#define __HAL_FDCAN_CLEAR_FLAG(__HANDLE__, __FLAG__)             \
do{                                                              \
    ((__HANDLE__)->Instance->IR) = ((__FLAG__) & FDCAN_IR_MASK); \
    FDCAN_CCU->IR = (((__FLAG__) & CCU_IR_MASK) >> 30);          \
  }while(0)

#define __HAL_FDCAN_ENABLE_IT(__HANDLE__, __INTERRUPT__)             \
  do{                                                                \
    (__HANDLE__)->Instance->IE |= ((__INTERRUPT__) & FDCAN_IR_MASK); \
    FDCAN_CCU->IE |= (((__INTERRUPT__) & CCU_IR_MASK) >> 30);        \
  }while(0)


**Code part:**
I observe following code part present within HAL_FDCAN_IRQHandler() function’s body:

  TxEventFifoITs = hfdcan->Instance->IR & FDCAN_TX_EVENT_FIFO_MASK;
  TxEventFifoITs &= hfdcan->Instance->IE;
  RxFifo0ITs = hfdcan->Instance->IR & FDCAN_RX_FIFO0_MASK;
  RxFifo0ITs &= hfdcan->Instance->IE;
  RxFifo1ITs = hfdcan->Instance->IR & FDCAN_RX_FIFO1_MASK;
  RxFifo1ITs &= hfdcan->Instance->IE;
…
  /* Tx event FIFO interrupts management **************************************/
  if (TxEventFifoITs != 0U)
  {
    /* Clear the Tx Event FIFO flags */
    __HAL_FDCAN_CLEAR_FLAG(hfdcan, TxEventFifoITs);

    /* Tx Event FIFO Callback */
    HAL_FDCAN_TxEventFifoCallback(hfdcan, TxEventFifoITs);
  }

  /* Rx FIFO 0 interrupts management ******************************************/
  if (RxFifo0ITs != 0U)
  {
    /* Clear the Rx FIFO 0 flags */
    __HAL_FDCAN_CLEAR_FLAG(hfdcan, RxFifo0ITs);

    /* Rx FIFO 0 Callback */
    HAL_FDCAN_RxFifo0Callback(hfdcan, RxFifo0ITs);
  }

  /* Rx FIFO 1 interrupts management ******************************************/
  if (RxFifo1ITs != 0U)
  {
    /* Clear the Rx FIFO 1 flags */
    __HAL_FDCAN_CLEAR_FLAG(hfdcan, RxFifo1ITs);

    /* Rx FIFO 1 Callback */
    HAL_FDCAN_RxFifo1Callback(hfdcan, RxFifo1ITs);
  }

Please share your views.

Thanks,
Rajeev

